`dispatchAsyncJob`/`waitAsyncJob` re-implement the exact host-function half of the `runAsync` contract that the package already ships as `AsyncEnginePool.attachMainRuntime` (same `__jsrDispatchHost`/`__jsrWaitHost` names, same `__jsError` sentinel shape, same envelope `{ok, result, error}` shape, even the same duplicated arg-validation message):

```dart
// async_job_pool.dart:190 — adapter-owned copy
String dispatchAsyncJob(AsyncJobPool pool, String argsJson, ...) {
  final args = jsonDecode(argsJson);
  if (args is! List || args.length < 2 || args[0] is! String) {
    return jsonEncode({'__jsError': 'runAsync expects (function, args) — ...'});
  }
  ...
}
```

The asymmetry is that the **prelude JS half lives in the package** (`asyncJobPrelude`) while the **host-fn half lives here** — a jsr minor bump (this PR pins `^0.3.3`, so 0.3.x can move) that changes the prelude's host-fn expectations breaks this adapter at runtime instead of at compile time. Today the `async_job_pool_test.dart` suite pins the contract, so drift is caught — but the duplication itself is the debt.

The reason `attachMainRuntime` can't be used as-is is real (it only supports pool-level `AsyncDispatchContext`, while the adapter needs per-run script dir/params/overrides). Suggested resolution, pick one:

1. Extend jsr so `attachMainRuntime` accepts a per-dispatch context provider (or `dispatch` takes an optional context), then delete `dispatchAsyncJob`/`waitAsyncJob` and delegate; or
2. Keep the adapter but add a doc comment on `dispatchAsyncJob` stating it intentionally mirrors `AsyncEnginePool._dispatchHost`/`_waitHost` and must be updated in lockstep with `asyncJobPrelude` — plus an assertion-style test that fails loudly if the package prelude stops referencing `__jsrDispatchHost`/`__jsrWaitHost`.
"}

